Chapter 18 – part 1
Years ago.
The Appalachian forest did not sleep the way human places slept. It breathed. It watched. It opened paths for the ones it recognized and closed them on the ones it wanted to keep lost. Most people who entered the wrong stretch of it never came back with their minds intact. Spirits there could peel a soul down to trembling. Cryptids hunted with patience older than roads. Even the kinder fae were not safe in the way cities understood safety.
But Curtis was ten, Justice was nine, and the forest was not their nightmare.
It was home.

They moved through the trees like the land itself had agreed to lean out of their way.
“Hahaha! Catch me if you can, Curtis!”
Justice shot ahead between trunks and under low branches, barefoot, laughing so hard the sound scattered birds from the canopy. Curtis tore after him, long younger braids snapping behind him, mismatched eyes bright with the pure fury of a boy being outrun by someone shorter.
“Justice, you little—!”
They crashed through the forest at impossible child speed, skimming root and stone, leaping deadfall, sliding down banks of old leaves. To any ordinary eye they would have looked like blurs between the trees, two abandoned human boys who had no business being this comfortable in a place that ate grown men. But the woods knew them. Some of the watching things even shifted aside, amused or tolerant, as the pair carved their game through territory that should have killed them.
They ran until the run became enough.

Later, the fire was small and careful. They sat on opposite sides of it with stained fingers, eating berries pulled from a bush Justice had declared “probably not the evil ones.” The night insects sang. Somewhere deeper in the trees, something large moved and then decided they were not worth the trouble.
Justice leaned back on his hands, face lit orange by the flames.
“We’re going to live in the big city one day and make a name for ourselves. Right, Curtis?”
Curtis popped another berry into his mouth and shrugged like the future was already a road they owned.
“Sure we are, Justice. Just me and you against the world.” He glanced sideways, more thoughtful. “Do you think the others will be okay with us leaving?”
Justice laughed, easy and bright.
“Probably. We aren’t crashing through their homes anymore.”
That was true enough. The two of them had grown up between worlds that most humans only met in warnings: cryptids with old hunger, fae with older manners, spirits that could unspool a mind for walking into the wrong clearing at the wrong hour. Some of those beings would have killed almost anyone else. A few had instead become the strange architecture of the boys’ childhood — neighbors of a kind, hazards of another, part of the only home either of them still claimed after being abandoned into the trees.
Justice’s smile sharpened with that particular fire he always got when talking about the outside world.
“We can stick it to the people that abandoned us here, right, Curtis?!”
Curtis grinned back, all teeth and loyalty.
“You know it, Justice!”
They scrambled up from the fire and started dancing in the dirt like the forest was a drum. Two scrawny boys beating their chests, shouting at the canopy, laughing so hard it turned into breathless stupidity. No audience but the trees. No future yet but the one they were already promising each other.
Just me and you against the world.

In the present, asleep in an Ikebukuro bedroom with a headless woman beside him, Curtis’s face twitched once against the pillow.
The dream held.
The fire kept crackling.
And Justice, still nine years old forever in this room of memory, kept laughing like he had never learned how to leave.
Curtis woke like a man breaking the surface of deep water.
His breathing came hard and uneven, chest rising too fast under the black compression shirt. Tears were already streaming down his face before he fully understood he was awake — hot, silent, the kind that belonged to a dream that had not stayed politely inside sleep. For a second the ceiling of the Ikebukuro bedroom and the canopy of Appalachian trees occupied the same air. Then the forest slipped, and only the room remained.
He sat up sharply, long black braids falling forward over his shoulders, one hand pressing hard to his face as if he could shove the wetness back in by force. A rough breath dragged out of him. Then another.
Beside him, the mattress shifted.
Celty was already awake to the change. Black smoke rose in a sudden, attentive ribbon from the empty space above her collar as she pushed up onto one elbow, the covers falling around her pale shoulders and black nightwear. For a moment she only watched him — the rigid line of his back, the tremor he was failing to hide, the way his body had left sleep like it was fleeing something.
Her real voice came through the private channel low and immediate.
“…Curtis.”
Not a demand. A fixed point.
“You’re awake. You’re here.” A short pause as more black smoke curled into the dim room. “Whatever followed you out of sleep can stay there. Breathe.”
She did not grab him. She also did not look away. One gloved hand rested on the sheet between them, available without forcing contact, while the headless woman in his bed waited to see whether he would let the dream finish taking him — or come all the way back to the room where he had not been alone.
Curtis dragged the heel of his hand across his face, long black braids shifting as he forced his breathing down into something closer to normal. The tears still left a shine at the edges of his mismatched eyes, but his voice came out flat on purpose.
“I’m fine. I was just having a dream. Or a memory of another time…” A short pause. Softer, despite the attempt at control. “It was nice.”
Celty remained propped on one elbow beside him, black smoke rising in a slower, more skeptical ribbon from the empty space above her collar. She looked at the wetness he had not managed to erase completely, then at the residual tension still locked across his shoulders.
Her real voice came through the private channel quiet and unconvinced.
“…Nice things don’t usually wake people like that.”
A short pause.
“You don’t have to dress it up for me. If it was nice, it can still hurt. Those two are not opposites.” The smoke drifted slightly toward him in the dim room. “Especially when the nice part belongs to a time that can’t answer you back.”
She left the observation there, no demand for the whole dream, only the clear refusal to accept I’m fine as the complete report.
“If you want to leave it as ‘nice,’ I’ll accept that for now. But you don’t have to sit there alone with it just because the memory smiled before it cut you.”
Curtis sat with his back against the headboard, long black braids falling forward over the black compression shirt, eyes fixed on some middle distance beyond the foot of the bed. For a while he only breathed. Then, because she had not pushed and because the dream was still too close to pretend it had been nothing, he started talking.
“I was ten. Justice was nine. We were in the forest.” A faint, rough breath left him. “Not running from anything. Just running. He kept yelling for me to catch him, laughing like the trees belonged to him. We were fast back then. Faster than we had any right to be. The woods… let us. Most people couldn’t have lasted out there. Cryptids. Fae. Spirits that could strip a mind down for crossing the wrong ground. But we’d grown up in it. Made friends with some of what lived there. To us it was just home.”
His fingers curled loosely in the sheet.
“After a while we stopped by a fire and ate berries. Justice started talking about the future like it was already waiting for us. Said we were going to live in the big city and make a name for ourselves. Just me and him against the world.” Curtis’s mouth twitched, not quite a smile. “I asked if the others would be okay with us leaving. He laughed. Said they probably would, since we weren’t crashing through their homes anymore.”
A longer silence stretched under the words.
“Then he said we could stick it to the people who abandoned us there. And I said, you know it. We got up and danced around the fire like idiots. Beating our chests. Shouting at the canopy. No audience. No plan. Just two abandoned boys promising each other a life big enough to make the leaving mean something.”
Curtis finally blinked, eyes still wet at the corners.
“…That’s all. It was nice. That’s what makes it worse.”
Celty had not interrupted once. Black smoke rose in a slow, attentive column from the empty space above her collar, the private channel held open and silent until he finished. When her real voice came, it was low and careful, stripped of every urge to analyze him into something more manageable.
“…He sounds like he made the forest bearable.”
A short pause.
“Not by denying what it was. By giving you a future inside it. A city. A name. A version of ‘after’ that still had both of you in the frame.” The smoke drifted sideways in the dim room. “No wonder a kind memory wakes you like a wound. It isn’t only grief for how he died. It’s grief for the world the two of you were still building when you were too young to know some promises can be broken by the person who made them.”
Her gloved hand rested on the sheet between them, available without forcing contact.
“Thank you for telling me. You didn’t have to reduce it to ‘nice’ to make it safer. I can hold the whole thing — the running, the fire, the dancing, and the fact that it hurts precisely because it was good.”
Curtis’s expression tightened as the thought caught up with him, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt.
“…Sorry if I’m insulting you.” His voice dropped. “Here I am able to recount my memories. And you only have the ones after you lost your head. My apologies, Celty.”
Celty went still beside him. Black smoke rose in a thinner, more complicated ribbon from the empty space above her collar. For a moment the private channel held only quiet, as if she were measuring whether the apology was necessary or simply another way for him to turn his own pain into caution.
When her real voice came, it was calm and clear.
“You’re not insulting me.”
A short pause.
“My missing years are not a wound you reopen by having a childhood you can still name. They existed. Then they were taken. That is my history, not a standard you failed by remembering your own.” The smoke drifted slightly toward him. “If anything, it would be stranger if you tried to protect me from the fact that you can still see your past in color.”
Another quieter beat.
“I envy the clarity sometimes. Not the suffering inside it — the continuity. Knowing who stood beside you when you were ten. Knowing the shape of a promise made at a fire.” Her gloved hand remained on the sheet between them. “But envy is not the same as offense. You don’t have to starve your memories to be considerate of mine.”
The empty line of her neck inclined toward him.
“Keep telling the truth when you can stand to. I asked to stay by you. That includes the parts where your past is fuller than mine.”
Curtis looked at her for a long moment in the dim bedroom, long black braids resting against his shoulders, the residual wetness from the dream still faintly catching the low light.
“…Celty. Do you age?”
Black smoke rose once above her collar. Her real voice came through the private channel without hesitation.
“No.”
Something in Curtis’s face changed — not relief exactly, but a quieter, almost fragile recognition. A small smile pulled at the edge of his mouth, tired and careful all at once.
“…I see. So…” He swallowed once. “Will you still be my friend when everyone else ages out?”
The question landed with the full weight of the roof conversation behind it. Not casual. Not hypothetical. A man who expected to stop at twenty-five asking the one person in the room who might still be standing when every human bond he made had finished its natural term.
Celty did not answer immediately. The black smoke above her collar slowed, then curled inward as if the question had reached past convenience and into the same long arithmetic she rarely let herself examine too directly.
When her real voice returned, it was lower and more deliberate than before.
“…That is not a small thing to ask.”
A short pause.
“I have already watched human time behave differently from mine. I know what it is to remain while other people move through years I do not spend the same way.” The smoke drifted toward him. “So if you are asking whether I am capable of still being here when the faces around you change… yes. I am.”
Another heavier beat.
“If you are asking whether I will promise to stay your friend through all of that—” her gloved hand shifted slightly on the sheet, “—then I won’t make a vow I haven’t tested against years neither of us have lived yet. But I also won’t insult you by pretending the question is meaningless.”
The empty line of her neck inclined toward him.
“I can remain. And if the bond is real enough to survive what time does to everyone else, then yes, Curtis… I would still be your friend when the rest of the room has gone.”
Curtis gave a small nod against the headboard, long black braids shifting over the black compression shirt.
“…Alright. That’s fair. Hopefully the answer changes by the time my list is done.”
Black smoke rose more sharply above Celty’s collar.
“What list?”
Curtis’s eyes slid away toward the sealed boxes in the corner, then toward the triple screens, then nowhere in particular.
“It’s nothing. Just things Justice and I made. Things we were going to do.” A short, almost offhand pause. “The list is almost done. College and detective work.”
Celty’s real voice came quieter.
“So what happens when the list is done?”
Curtis’s mouth tightened. The temperature of the conversation changed by a fraction — not dramatic, not openly despairing, just closed in a way that did not match the words.
“It’s nothing.”
He left it there. No joke to soften it. No explanation to make it safe. Just the same flat seal he used when a door had been opened one inch too far.
Celty did not press.
She remained still beside him on the bed, black smoke thinning into a slower, more troubled ribbon as the private channel stayed open and unused. Outwardly, she accepted the shutdown. Inwardly, the worry landed hard and immediate.
The list is almost done.
College. Detective work.
Then — nothing.
It might have been only grief talking. Only the voice of a boy who had built a future for two and did not know how to revise it for one. But the tone under it’s nothing had not sounded empty. It had sounded terminal in a quiet way — like a man who had been completing inherited tasks and had not yet decided whether life after the final item was a continuation or merely the end of an assignment.
She kept that fear to herself.
For now, she only sat with him in the dim room, one gloved hand still near enough to reach if the silence turned worse, and filed the list under the growing set of things about Curtis Nightstar that could not be left unsupervised forever.
The phone rang against the quiet of the bedroom.
Curtis blinked once, still half-anchored in the conversation about lists and years, then reached for it on the side table. Rio’s name lit the screen. He answered with a voice carefully flattened back toward normal.
“…Hey.”
Rio’s tone on the other end was lighter than the room he was sitting in, morning-bright and practical.
“Curtis? Classes are canceled today. I just saw the notice.”
He leaned his head back against the headboard, long black braids shifting over the black compression shirt.
“I see. Thanks for the heads-up.”
A short pause on the line. Then Rio again, a little more hopeful around the edges.
“Are you free today?”
Curtis’s mismatched eyes flicked sideways toward Celty before he answered — toward the black smoke rising from the empty space above her collar, the fact of her still being in his bed, the unfinished weight of the night.
“…I’m not sure yet. I’ll let you know.”
Rio accepted it with only a small delay.
“Okay. Just text me if you are. No pressure.” Another beat, softer. “I’m glad you’re up, at least.”
“Yeah. Talk later.”
He ended the call and let the phone rest against his thigh. The bedroom felt different with Rio’s morning voice still dissipating into a space that had spent the night on grief, shared beds, and questions about who would remain when everyone else aged out.
Celty had not moved much during the call. Black smoke curled once above her collar, slower now, attentive. When her real voice came through the private channel, it was dry around the edges but not unkind.
“…She sounds fond of you.”
A short pause.
“And you sound like a man who just realized his canceled school day is already occupied by more than one kind of complication.” The smoke drifted slightly toward the phone. “You don’t have to explain her to me. But if you’re going to keep telling people you’ll ‘let them know,’ you should decide what the truth is before the next message arrives.”
Her gloved hand rested loosely against the sheet between them.
“For what it’s worth… I didn’t come here to take the whole day from you by default. If you need to go, go. If you want me to leave, say so.”
Curtis shook his head once, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt as he set the phone aside.
“No. I just wanna stay inside during the day.” A short pause. He glanced toward Celty, mismatched eyes still heavy from the morning’s residue. “By the way — it’s that girl Rio. From when you first met me.”
The reference landed cleanly enough. The rooftop. The jump. The foreigner who caught a falling girl and cracked pavement doing it. The first time the Black Rider had looked at him and started filing him under something more complicated than passerby.
Celty’s black smoke rose in a slow, recognizing ribbon from the empty space above her collar. Her real voice came through the private channel quieter, more thoughtful.
“…The one you saved.”
Not a question. A placement.
“I remember. She walked away after. You stayed in the street long enough for me to ask why you’d done it.” A short pause. “So she still reaches for you.”
The smoke drifted slightly toward the phone on the bed.
“That makes sense. People don’t usually forget the hand that catches them in midair.” Another beat, less analytical and more present. “If you’re staying in today, then the message you owe her can wait until you know what kind of day this actually is. You don’t have to rearrange yourself for every soft request just because you know how to answer them.”
Her gloved hand rested near his on the sheet.
“For now, inside is fine.”
Curtis shook his head a little faster this time, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt as if the implication itself needed swatting away.
“No. It’s nothing like that with her.” His voice stayed even, practical. “She recognized me the following day in class. I got caught up playing music with these people busking, and while I was playing my hood came down and my braids fell out. Made it easy to tell later who I was. After class we exchanged numbers.”
He left it there — rooftop to street performance to classroom recognition to a phone contact. No confession of deeper entanglement. No claim that the story was finished either.
Celty listened in silence, black smoke rising in a slow, considering ribbon from the empty space above her collar. When her real voice came through the private channel, it carried a dry note of recognition more than judgment.
“…So she caught the masked version first, then the school version after.”
A short pause.
“That would do it. Braids are a poor disguise once they’ve already been seen under streetlights.” The smoke drifted slightly. “And exchanging numbers after a rescue is a very ordinary human consequence. It doesn’t require a dramatic reading… even if the way she calls first thing in the morning suggests she didn’t file you under ‘stranger who helped once.’”
Another quieter beat.
“I’m not accusing you of anything, Curtis. I’m only saying the people you pull out of the air tend not to settle for distance afterward.” Her gloved hand rested loosely near his on the sheet. “Whether it’s ‘nothing like that’ or not, she still reaches for you. That much is already true.”
Curtis only shrugged, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt, as if the whole category of implication could be left on the sheet between them and ignored.
Celty accepted the shrug in silence.
Black smoke rose thinner from the empty space above her collar while, privately, her thoughts turned on her with immediate irritation.
Why are you lecturing him about another girl?
The question arrived sharp and unwelcome. She was not his girlfriend. She had no claim on his mornings, his phone, or the people who had gotten there before last night. One shared bed, one long confession, one almost-tender rooftop conversation about time and aging did not make her the arbiter of who was allowed to call him when classes were canceled. She had a life already. A complicated one. A man across the city who still believed he understood the shape of her devotion.
And yet she had heard Rio’s name and immediately started measuring distance, attachment, consequence.
That is not your place.
The correction did not fully land. Knowing she had no official right to the reaction did not remove the reaction itself. If anything, the lack of claim only made the impulse more revealing — and more dangerous. She had asked to stay by him. She had slept against him. She had listened to the forest and the list and the fear of everyone aging out. Of course some part of her bristled at another soft voice reaching into the morning after.
That did not make the bristling righteous.
It only made it honest.
Celty kept the private channel closed on all of that and simply sat there, helmetless in his bed, watching a man who had shrugged as if none of the surrounding complexity belonged to him — while she quietly failed to convince herself that none of it was beginning to belong to her.
Curtis leaned his head back against the headboard, long black braids falling over one shoulder as he stared at the ceiling for a second before speaking.
“Relationships get messy quick. I’m not fully interested in one at the moment. Even if Rio sees me a certain way… I’m simply not interested.”
The practical edge of it held for half a breath. Then his mismatched eyes lowered and settled on Celty — on the black smoke rising from the empty space above her collar, on the woman who had stayed through dream-wreckage, old forests, and questions about who would remain when everyone else aged out.
“…But.” His voice quieted. “If I had to choose to be with someone… I would hope they are as understanding like you are, Celty.”
The line landed in the room with more weight than a shrug could carry.
Celty went very still. Black smoke above her collar tightened, then rose in an uneven ribbon as the private channel stayed silent longer than was comfortable. When her real voice finally came through, it was careful to the point of strain.
“…You shouldn’t say that so easily.”
A short pause.
“I’m not a clean example of understanding. I’m someone who stayed the night, asked questions I only half had the right to ask, and woke up listening to another woman call you with more interest than I was prepared to hear.” The smoke drifted sideways. “That is not neutrality. That is already complication.”
Another heavier beat.
“And you know I am not free of entanglement. Saying you would hope for someone like me does not land like a compliment in a vacuum. It lands like a door being tested.” Her gloved hand flexed once against the sheet. “I can accept that you aren’t looking for a relationship right now. That part is clear. But don’t hold me up as the shape of a future choice unless you understand what that does to the person hearing it.”
The empty line of her neck inclined toward him, softer despite the warning.
“…I am glad you think I’m understanding. Just… be careful with the rest of that sentence.”
Curtis’s mouth tugged into something almost dry, long black braids shifting as he met the empty space above her collar without flinching.
“Justice used to tell me the same thing when I spoke to women.” A short pause. “Although I’m not testing a door of any kind. See it as you may… but you’re the one who stayed the night and just finished lecturing me about another girl. Are you sure you haven’t been testing a door since you met me?”
The question hit cleanly because it was fair.
Black smoke burst upward from Celty’s collar in a sharp, betrayed little spike before she forced it under control. For a moment the private channel held nothing but the static of someone who had been accurately caught. She had stayed. She had asked to stay by him, not merely near him. She had measured Rio’s morning voice like it mattered. She had compared, considered, lingered, and then tried to lecture from a position she did not officially occupy.
He was right.
When her real voice finally came through, it was lower and stripped of easy defense.
“…That’s an annoying observation.”
A short pause.
“And not an incorrect one.” The smoke thinned, then drifted toward him almost reluctantly. “I keep telling myself I came here for clarity. For answers. Because you can hear me. Because the night on the street left something unfinished. All of that is true. It is also not the complete list.”
Another heavier beat.
“I have been testing the edge of something since the first time you answered my actual voice instead of a screen. I don’t like admitting that. Especially not when my life is already supposed to be spoken for.” Her gloved hand flexed once against the sheet. “So yes. Maybe I lectured you about another girl because some part of me was already standing too close to a door I have not formally agreed to open.”
The empty line of her neck inclined toward him, less armored than before.
“…Don’t look so satisfied about it.”
Celty’s answer to his self-satisfaction was immediate and physical.
She punched his shoulder — not hard enough to injure, but firm enough to make the point — black smoke spiking above her collar in pure indignation. Curtis let out a low chuckle, long black braids shifting against the pillow as he tipped his head back.
“I was only kidding. But hey… since you said it—”
He did not get to finish the sentence.
Celty moved with the kind of speed that made arguments about dignity irrelevant. The mattress dipped, covers tangled, and in one decisive shift she had him flat to the bed with her weight settled over his hips, knees bracketing him, gloved hands planting against the black compression shirt over his chest. Black smoke poured upward from the empty space above her collar in a flustered, aggressive ribbon.
Her real voice came through the private channel sharp and close.
“Do not.”
A short, heated pause.
“Do not finish that sentence like you won something.” The smoke drifted down around them as she kept him pinned, more flustered than truly furious. “You baited me into saying it out loud and now you’re smiling like the confession was a prize. That is exactly why Justice used to warn you.”
Another beat, lower, the awareness of their position arriving half a second late and making the channel tighten.
“…And if you point out how this looks, I am throwing you off the bed.”
Curtis’s chuckle thinned into something more careful as the reality of their position caught up with him. Long black braids spilled across the pillow, mismatched eyes flicking up toward the black smoke above her collar.
“…Well. You’re on top of my—”
He did not need to finish.
Celty felt it.
The shift of his body under hers was unmistakable, heat and firmness answering the straddle with a honesty neither of them had formally invited into the argument. Black smoke erupted upward from the empty space above her collar in a sharp, mortified surge. For a second she went completely still on him, gloved hands still planted against his chest, the private channel filling with the kind of silence that meant her thoughts had all tried to leave the room at once.
Then her real voice came through, tight and dangerous with embarrassment.
“…Curtis.”
A short pause.
“That had better be an involuntary reaction and not commentary.” The smoke curled hard around them as she remained exactly where she was, caught between the urge to move and the fact that moving would only acknowledge what she had already felt. “You do not get to bait me into a confession, smile about it, and then make this—” a faint, frustrated drag of breath, “—into additional evidence.”
Another beat, lower and more flustered.
“I am choosing to ignore that for five seconds. If you say one smug word while I do, I am leaving you on this mattress alone with your consequences.”
Curtis only shrugged, the picture of a man refusing to die of embarrassment on principle.
“Welp. I’m going for a smoke.”
With relative ease — the kind that made the earlier floor-spill look like a different species of reaction — he took her by the waist and set her aside on the mattress as if the whole crisis of proximity were a logistical problem with a simple solution. Long black braids shifted against the black compression shirt as he sat up, already reaching toward the side table.
“Celty, you do realize you’re very attractive. It’s only normal my body would react that way.”
He said it flatly, almost practically, then grabbed his cigarettes and was already moving for the door before the sentence finished settling. A moment later his footsteps headed toward the roof access, leaving the bedroom suddenly wider and a great deal quieter.
Celty remained where he had placed her on the bed.
Black smoke exploded upward from the empty space above her collar in a chaotic ribbon, then tangled on itself like it could not decide whether to be furious, flustered, or insultingly pleased. The private channel stayed sealed for a full second while she processed the sequence: confession bait, straddle, unmistakable physical reaction, casual lift, blunt compliment, exit for a cigarette.
When her real voice finally came through, it was aimed at the empty doorway and distinctly aggrieved.
“…You do not get to say that and leave.”
A short, heated pause.
“‘Very attractive.’ ‘Only normal.’ As if that settles the matter. As if you can just set me aside, announce a biological inevitability, and go smoke on the roof like the morning is ordinary.” The smoke dragged hard toward the door. “Curtis Nightstar, that was not an exit. That was a retreat dressed up as composure.”
Another beat, lower, more reluctant.
“…And stop being right about the reaction. That makes it worse.”
She sat there in the disordered covers a second longer, black nightwear and rising shadow, then pushed off the bed with the clear intent of following him up into the air where he was not going to get the last word for free.
Celty’s real voice snapped through the private channel as she left the bedroom.
“Hey—wait! I said if you said something stupid I would leave! You don’t get to reverse that!”
Black smoke surged around her body in a controlled sweep, the nightwear dissolving and reforming into her default black riding suit with practiced speed. Helmet still left behind in the flat, she took the stairs hard, shadow trailing from the empty space above her collar like a banner of pure indignation.
On the roof, the morning air was cooler and cleaner. Curtis was already at the ledge, cigarette lit, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt as city haze stretched out below. When he noticed her, he simply raised two fingers in a small, unbothered wave.
Celty stopped just short of the ledge and stared at him.
Black smoke rose in a sharp, offended column. Her real voice came through low and lethal with disbelief.
“…Did you just wave at me?”
A short pause.
“You baited a confession, got straddled, made a biological announcement, set me aside like luggage, and now you’re on the roof waving like this is a friendly smoke break.” The smoke dragged toward him in the wind. “I am not here for the scenic version of your retreat, Curtis. You do not get to flip the terms, leave the room, and then greet me like I came up for sightseeing.”
Another beat, tighter.
“Put the cigarette in your mouth and prepare to finish the conversation you started by running away from it.”
Curtis tilted his head, cigarette resting between his fingers, long black braids shifting in the roof wind as if her entire ascent were an interesting weather change rather than a pursuit.
“Well… what’s there to finish?” A short pause. He tipped his chin toward the empty stretch of ledge beside him. “Come stand beside me, Celty. I just wanted to have a cigarette.”
The simplicity of it was almost offensive.
Black smoke rose hard from the empty space above Celty’s collar, caught and torn sideways by the morning air. For a second she remained where she was, riding suit stark against the rooftop concrete, every line of her posture still loaded with the argument she had carried up the stairs. Then the private channel opened, her real voice lower than the climb had promised and more conflicted than she wanted it to be.
“…You are unbelievable.”
A short pause.
“There is plenty to finish. You do not get to detonate a morning like that and then reduce it to nicotine and scenery.” The smoke drifted toward the ledge despite her tone. “And yet you’re standing there asking me to come stand beside you as if that isn’t another door being left open on purpose.”
Another beat. She moved anyway, closing the distance until she stood at the ledge with him, close enough that the wind pushed her shadow-smoke across his sleeve.
“…Fine. Smoke your cigarette.” Quieter, almost grudging. “But don’t mistake me coming over here for the conversation being over. It just means I’m willing to be angry next to you instead of downstairs alone.”
Celty stood beside him at the ledge for a stretch of quiet, black smoke trailing from the empty space above her collar and breaking apart in the morning wind. Below them, Ikebukuro moved like it had not spent the night rearranging either of their lives. Curtis smoked. She did not look at the cigarette so much as at the line of his profile, the braids, the residual calm of a man who kept surviving his own intensity by stepping sideways into ordinary gestures.
When her real voice came through the private channel, it was steadier than the stairs had been.
“…I keep trying to treat this like a series of isolated incidents.”
A short pause.
“You hearing me. The race. The extraction. Last night. The dream. The list. This morning. Every time, I tell myself there is a clean explanation that leaves my life intact and your distance intact and nobody standing on a roof afterward pretending a cigarette can reset the temperature.” The smoke drifted across his sleeve again. “But it isn’t isolated. I stayed. I asked to stay by you. I reacted to another girl’s voice on your phone. I pinned you to a mattress because you made me admit too much, and then I followed you up here because letting you walk away felt worse than being embarrassed.”
Another heavier beat.
“That means something is already happening, Curtis. Whether you want a relationship or not. Whether I am supposed to be available for one or not.” Her gloved hands rested against the ledge. “I can accept uncertainty. What I can’t accept is us keeping up the performance that this is still just coincidence, convenience, and shared insomnia.”
The empty line of her neck turned toward him.
“So if you’re going to stand there and smoke, fine. But stop acting like the only reasonable version of me is the one that climbs back down and pretends the door was never open.”
Curtis took another drag from the cigarette and let the smoke leave him slowly, long black braids shifting in the roof wind as he kept his eyes on the city rather than on her.
“You shouldn’t keep that all in and let it explode the way you do, Celty.” A short, dry pause. “…I know. That’s rich coming from me.”
The self-awareness sat between them without excuse. He did not dress it up further. He had spent the night proving the same flaw in a different direction — rationing truth, delaying admission, then putting too much into a room all at once and calling the aftermath honesty.
Celty remained at the ledge beside him, black smoke thinning into a slower ribbon above her collar. When her real voice came through the private channel, it carried less heat than before and more tired recognition.
“…It is rich.”
A short pause.
“You’re standing there diagnosing my pressure buildup when half the reason I’m on this roof is that you keep storing things behind blunt sentences until the lock fails.” The smoke drifted across his sleeve. “Still. You’re not wrong. I do hold things until they come out sideways. Anger. Attachment. Fear of what it means that your voice is easier to answer than the life I already have.”
Another quieter beat.
“Maybe that is why this keeps escalating. You bottle. I bottle. Then one of us opens a door too fast and the other one either falls off the bed or climbs three flights of stairs to argue about it.” Her gloved hands rested against the ledge. “If we’re going to keep ending up in the same rooms, we can’t both keep treating emotional honesty like an emergency exit.”
The empty line of her neck inclined toward him.
“…I’ll try not to explode it out of you next time. If you try not to disappear to a rooftop the second it gets real.”
Curtis chuckled under his breath, the sound low and almost ordinary as he tapped ash from the cigarette over the ledge. Long black braids shifted against the black compression shirt in the morning wind.
“But I really did want a smoke.” A short pause, more practical than defensive. “A bad habit I had before coming here is I used to smoke weed. So I cope with cigarettes. This isn’t L.A. — I’ll get arrested and put in jail. I came here to enjoy Japan, not go to jail over a stupid habit.”
The confession was so bluntly logistical that it briefly short-circuited the heavier temperature of the roof.
Celty stood beside him in silence for a second, black smoke rising in a thinner, almost disbelieving ribbon from the empty space above her collar. When her real voice came through the private channel, it carried a dry edge of reluctant amusement under the residual tension.
“…Of course.”
A short pause.
“I climb up here ready to finish an emotional confrontation, and your defense is controlled substance pragmatism.” The smoke drifted sideways in the wind. “You fled a charged room, yes. You also fled it specifically to stand in the open air and feed a substitute habit so you wouldn’t end up in a Japanese holding cell over California coping mechanisms. That is… extremely you.”
Another beat, quieter, the sharpness loosening.
“I’m not sure whether that makes the exit more forgivable or more annoying.” Her gloved hands rested against the ledge. “At least it wasn’t only retreat. I’ll give you that much. Just don’t expect ‘I needed a cigarette because weed is illegal here’ to cover every difficult conversation from now on.”
The empty line of her neck inclined toward him, almost resigned.
“…Smoke your legal regret, then. But you’re still not done talking after it.”
Curtis laughed once, softer than the subject deserved, and flicked the spent cigarette out over the safe edge of the roof’s dead zone. Then, without making a speech of it, he took Celty’s gloved hand. Long black braids shifted against the black compression shirt as his mismatched eyes met the empty space above her collar.
“So. What do you want to do about this.” A short pause. “You wanted to talk about this subject, so…?”
The question was plain. No retreat in it now. No cigarette to hide behind. Just the open fact of their joined hands and the morning air around a connection neither of them could keep calling coincidence.
Celty’s black smoke rose hard, then steadied. For a moment the private channel held only the weight of a woman being asked to name a direction when every available road already had consequences attached. When her real voice came through, it was low and carefully stripped of theatrics.
“…I don’t know if I get a clean answer yet.”
A short pause. Her fingers closed around his anyway.
“Part of me wants to pretend we can leave this on the roof and climb back down as something simpler. Friends who hear each other. People who show up when the night gets too honest. That would be the neat version.” The smoke drifted across their joined hands. “But I already stayed. I already asked to sleep beside you. I already reacted to another girl’s voice like it mattered. So neat is probably a lie.”
Another heavier beat.
“What I want is time that isn’t stolen from denial. I want to keep talking without performing distance. I want to be near you without immediately turning every point of contact into a verdict about the rest of my life.” Her grip tightened once. “I am not free in the convenient way. You know that. Which means anything I do about this has to be careful, or it becomes just another kind of damage.”
The empty line of her neck inclined toward him.
“So what I want, right now, is not a grand conclusion. It’s honesty without the emergency exit. Keep holding my hand. Keep standing here. And don’t ask me to define the whole future before I’ve finished admitting the present is already different.”
A final quieter line.
“…Can that be enough for this morning?”